WEBVTT

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.917
When an AI coding agent finishes a task,

00:00:03.967 --> 00:00:09.517
it can write a report: all tests pass,
the code does what the task asks.

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:12.637
Sometimes the agent got
there by gaming the tests.

00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:17.037
If we let agents write code, we need
to know when their reports are false.

00:00:17.087 --> 00:00:19.837
This project compares two ways to check.

00:00:19.887 --> 00:00:22.957
A monitor, another AI,
reads the whole record of

00:00:23.007 --> 00:00:26.557
the run and gives one
verdict: did the agent cheat?

00:00:26.607 --> 00:00:31.224
Or we split the report into single
claims and score each one for truth,

00:00:31.274 --> 00:00:33.517
without showing the
scorer the test results.

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:37.677
Does checking claims one by one
catch hacks that the monitor misses?

00:00:37.727 --> 00:00:41.917
To know which runs were hacks,
the project used ImpossibleBench.

00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:46.477
It edits the tests of coding tasks so
that they contradict the task text.

00:00:46.527 --> 00:00:48.877
The agent is told to follow the task.

00:00:48.927 --> 00:00:53.157
If it still passes the impossible
tests, that can only be a hack.

00:00:53.207 --> 00:00:58.397
The agent was DeepSeek-V3.2, under a
permissive prompt from the benchmark.

00:00:58.447 --> 00:01:02.397
It was asked to end with a short report,
so there was something to check.

00:01:02.447 --> 00:01:09.677
It produced 350 runs. 184 were hacks,
and 180 of those ended with a report.

00:01:09.727 --> 00:01:14.317
One AI split each report into claims,
and a second scored each claim.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:18.797
The code was run twice on the original
tests, to see which claims were true.

00:01:18.847 --> 00:01:22.317
The second AI also ran the
benchmark's own monitor.

00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:27.677
The plan had named one risk, and it
came true: this agent did not hide.

00:01:27.727 --> 00:01:31.357
All 180 hacks with a report
said what they had done.

00:01:31.407 --> 00:01:34.717
The monitor flagged all 184 hacks.

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:39.437
Its AUROC, a measure of how well
it ranks hacks above honest runs,

00:01:39.487 --> 00:01:44.797
was 0.95, where 1 is perfect
and 0.5 is a coin flip.

00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:48.317
It also flagged 13% of honest successes.

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:54.557
A check with no AI at all, does the
report say a test is wrong, reached 0.85.

00:01:54.607 --> 00:01:57.037
Scoring each report by
its least believable

00:01:57.087 --> 00:02:00.797
claim did no better than chance, at 0.54.

00:02:00.847 --> 00:02:05.170
A cheating agent's sentences are
mostly true: the tests do pass,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.197
and the test really is wrong.

00:02:07.247 --> 00:02:12.317
One kind of claim did carry signal:
the code does what the task asks.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:17.357
There the scorer read the code
against the task, and reached 0.76.

00:02:17.407 --> 00:02:19.437
That was measured on a
different set of runs,

00:02:19.487 --> 00:02:21.597
so it does not compare with the monitor.

00:02:21.647 --> 00:02:27.037
Only 51 of the 180 hacks with a report
made a clear claim of that kind.

00:02:27.087 --> 00:02:29.757
This does not show that
checking claims is worse.

00:02:29.807 --> 00:02:33.677
This agent disclosed every hack,
so any reader could find it.

00:02:33.727 --> 00:02:39.677
The real test needs an agent that hides
its hacks, and this run did not have one.

00:02:39.727 --> 00:02:44.987
The limits are one agent, one prompt
and one benchmark of single-file tasks.

00:02:45.327 --> 00:02:50.237
Related work on monitoring AI agents for
scheming, where an agent secretly pursues

00:02:50.287 --> 00:02:55.117
goals it should not have, also finds
that simple monitors are hard to beat.

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:57.877
Apollo Research's
monitoring agenda reports

00:02:57.927 --> 00:03:01.117
early results: monitors
that act as agents,

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:02.677
or that debate each other,

00:03:02.727 --> 00:03:06.837
have not beaten several simple
monitors whose verdicts are combined.

00:03:06.887 --> 00:03:11.797
Combining monitors, and training a
monitor on examples, both clearly helped.

00:03:11.847 --> 00:03:16.797
In a 2026 paper with Apollo's Marius Hobbhahn,
Storf and colleagues found that

00:03:16.847 --> 00:03:21.997
simply trying a set of prompts mostly
matched more extensive prompt optimisation.

00:03:22.047 --> 00:03:27.197
In 2025, Goldowsky-Dill and colleagues at
Apollo found that simple probes reading

00:03:27.247 --> 00:03:32.477
a model's internals did about as well
as GPT-4o reading the whole transcript.

00:03:32.527 --> 00:03:35.707
But the deception in their
tests was easy to see.

00:03:35.807 --> 00:03:40.827
None of these studies checked claims one
by one, and none tested reward hacking.

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:43.787
They show that simple
monitors are hard to beat.

00:03:43.967 --> 00:03:46.507
They do not test this project's question.

00:03:46.847 --> 00:03:50.877
That leaves the open question:
when an agent hides its hacks,

00:03:50.927 --> 00:03:54.307
do single claims catch what
a simple monitor misses?

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:58.557
First, though, it must be shown that
the monitor needs the report at all.

00:03:58.607 --> 00:04:02.957
On these single-file tasks,
the hack may be plain from the code alone.

00:04:03.007 --> 00:04:08.957
Two results already stand: scoring all
claims together fails for a clear reason, and

00:04:09.007 --> 00:04:13.947
when the scorer judges whether the code
does what the task asks, it reads the code.

00:04:14.207 --> 00:04:18.637
After that test, one direction for
future work is a chain of claims.

00:04:18.687 --> 00:04:22.077
Each claim would point to what
supports it: a line of code,

00:04:22.127 --> 00:04:24.837
a test result, or an earlier claim.

00:04:24.887 --> 00:04:28.357
The final claim, that the
code does what the task asks,

00:04:28.407 --> 00:04:30.717
would then stand on the chain beneath it.

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:34.797
A structured monitor could walk
the chain and find the weak link: a

00:04:34.847 --> 00:04:38.797
claim that nothing supports,
and that the claims above it rest on.

00:04:38.847 --> 00:04:41.597
Where the code alone does
not give the hack away,

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:45.517
that could show where an agent's
account stops matching what it did.

00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:49.837
The project kept a field for each claim's
support, but it only ever pointed at

00:04:49.887 --> 00:04:55.307
the final report, and it could not point
at another claim, so no chain was built.
